 {'kind': 'definition', 'title': 'Definition: Parasite Drag', 'img': 'PHAK_Fig5-7_form_drag', 'img2': 'PHAK_Fig5-8_interference_drag', 'cite': 'PHAK 5-6, PHAK Fig 5-7, PHAK Fig 5-8', 'code': K4, 'defsize': 16,
  'defs': [('Parasite drag', 'Parasite drag is comprised of all the forces that work to slow an aircraft\'s movement. As the term parasite implies, it is the drag that is not associated with the production of lift.')],
  'plain': 'Three types: form drag (shape), interference drag (airstreams meeting, like wing and fuselage), and skin friction (rough surfaces, rivets, dirt).',
  'notes': 'Form drag: the flat plate versus the streamlined shape. Interference drag: where the wing meets the fuselage or strut. Skin friction: rivets, paint chips, dirt.',
  'lp': {'teach': ['Read the definition (PHAK 5-6). The three types:',
     'Form drag: the portion generated by the aircraft\'s shape and airflow around it, such as cowlings and antennas (PHAK 5-6). Your note "Jeep vs. Ferrari" is a good analogy.',
     'Interference drag: comes from the intersection of airstreams that creates eddy currents, turbulence, or restricts smooth airflow, such as the wing root (PHAK 5-6). On the C172S, think of where the strut meets the wing.',
     'Skin friction drag: aerodynamic resistance from moving air contacting the surface. Flush rivets, a smooth finish, and a clean, waxed airplane reduce it (PHAK 5-6, PHAK 5-7).']}},
