 {'title': 'Induced Drag and the Drag Curve', 'img': 'PHAK_Fig5-6_drag_speed', 'cite': 'PHAK 5-7, PHAK 5-8, PHAK 5-5, PHAK Fig 5-6', 'code': K4,
  'bullets': ['Induced: comes from making lift', 'More AOA, more induced drag', 'Parasite rises with speed squared', 'L/D max: lowest total drag'],
  'notes': 'Induced drag is highest when slow, parasite drag is highest when fast. Where the two curves cross is the bottom of the total drag curve: L/D max.',
  'lp': {'teach': ['Induced drag is inherent whenever an airfoil is producing lift and is inseparable from the production of lift (PHAK 5-7).',
     'As AOA increases, induced drag increases. The lower the airspeed, the greater the AOA needed, so the greater the induced drag. Induced drag varies inversely with the square of the airspeed; parasite drag increases as the square of the airspeed (PHAK 5-8).',
     'At L/D max, total drag is at its minimum (PHAK 5-5).',
     'Note on your whiteboard: the PHAK says induced drag varies inversely with the square of airspeed and parasite drag increases as the square of airspeed, not just "inversely" and "directly."']}},
